<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Holiday is a 33-year-old singer with a severe opioid use disorder on buprenorphine and a moderate alcohol use disorder on acamprosate, who comes for her yearly preventive visit six weeks after her three-month review. Since that visit, when the recovery plan was adjusted after a brief return to use on tour, she has taken buprenorphine every day without a gap and has used no heroin or other opioids. The pharmacy now fills her prescription before each tour, and she carries a travel letter. She drinks one or two drinks on some performance nights and none on most other days. She still carries naloxone and fentanyl test strips. She meets her counselor weekly and her peer recovery coach by phone. Her mood is better, and her PHQ-9 today is 6. She has no new concerns. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 33</td></tr><tr><td>Opioid use</td><td>None since the return to use before the three-month visit; buprenorphine daily without gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>One or two drinks on some performance nights; AUDIT-C 3</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-9 6; item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Buprenorphine with naloxone 16 mg daily; acamprosate 666 mg three times daily; thiamine; naloxone spray on hand</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on paper in a private room and reviewed with her by Dr. Mayfield; kept in the confidential part of the record with her substance use treatment records. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair, and better than in the spring</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Supported by long-time musicians, a close friend, her counselor, and a peer recovery coach; grief for her mother still returns at times</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>No opioid use for six weeks on buprenorphine; AUDIT-C 3</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>About five cigarettes a day</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks most days; performing is physically demanding</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Irregular meals on performance days; eating more regularly at home</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Six to seven hours, better than at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>Sexually active; uses condoms; not planning a pregnancy now</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe at home; no one hurts or threatens her</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr><tr><td>Oral health</td><td>No dental visit in several years</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

